1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,027
Lesson 7.1.

2
00:00:02,407 --> 00:00:04,916
From factoring to order finding.

3
00:00:06,017 --> 00:00:10,159
Factoring a large number is
hard for digital computers,

4
00:00:10,159 --> 00:00:13,925
and much of cryptography
rests on that difficulty.

5
00:00:14,305 --> 00:00:18,598
Shor's algorithm showed that a quantum
computer would do it efficiently.

6
00:00:18,978 --> 00:00:22,479
In this unit we study an
alternative based on diffusion.

7
00:00:22,859 --> 00:00:26,087
But everything starts with
a classical trick: turning

8
00:00:26,087 --> 00:00:29,620
factoring into a problem of
finding the order of a number.

9
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:35,533
Take N = 299 and a
number a coprime with N.

10
00:00:35,913 --> 00:00:39,340
The powers of a modulo
N repeat in a cycle.

11
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:42,924
The order r is the length of that cycle:

12
00:00:42,924 --> 00:00:46,128
the smallest exponent such that a^r ≡ 1.

13
00:00:46,508 --> 00:00:49,752
For a = 2, the order is
one hundred thirty-two.

14
00:00:50,883 --> 00:00:57,252
If the order is even, x =
a^(r/2) satisfies x² ≡ 1.

15
00:00:57,632 --> 00:01:01,017
Then N divides (x − 1)(x + 1).

16
00:01:01,397 --> 00:01:07,271
If moreover x is not ± 1, N divides
neither factor separately, so the

17
00:01:07,271 --> 00:01:12,208
greatest common divisor of x
− 1 and N is a proper factor.

18
00:01:12,588 --> 00:01:16,761
For a = 2: x = 116, and the
greatest common divisor of one

19
00:01:16,761 --> 00:01:21,078
hundred fifteen and two hundred
ninety-nine is twenty-three.

20
00:01:21,458 --> 00:01:25,114
So two hundred ninety-nine is
thirteen times twenty-three.

21
00:01:26,217 --> 00:01:29,300
The Chinese remainder theorem
explains when it works.

22
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:33,017
Modulo N = p q, every number is a pair of

23
00:01:33,017 --> 00:01:36,437
remainders, one modulo p and one modulo q.

24
00:01:36,817 --> 00:01:39,484
A square root of one is a pair of signs.

25
00:01:39,864 --> 00:01:43,584
The trivial roots are plus-plus
and minus-minus; the mixed

26
00:01:43,584 --> 00:01:47,304
ones, plus-minus and minus-plus,
are the ones that factor.

27
00:01:47,684 --> 00:01:50,753
The trick fails only when
the power-of-two parts

28
00:01:50,753 --> 00:01:53,312
of the order coincide in all components.

29
00:01:54,417 --> 00:01:59,616
For a random a and m distinct
prime factors, a sufficient parity

30
00:01:59,616 --> 00:02:04,654
pattern has probability 1 − (m
+ 1)/2^m, at least one quarter.

31
00:02:05,034 --> 00:02:08,837
The exact law is stronger: at
least one half for semiprimes.

32
00:02:09,217 --> 00:02:14,423
In N = 299, counting all coprime
a, the trick works in one hundred

33
00:02:14,423 --> 00:02:19,787
ninety-eight of two hundred sixty-three
cases: seventy-five percent.

34
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:23,160
Repeating with another a
drives failure quickly to zero.

35
00:02:24,283 --> 00:02:27,092
All the hard work is in finding the order.

36
00:02:27,472 --> 00:02:31,630
Going through the powers one by
one costs up to r steps, and r

37
00:02:31,630 --> 00:02:35,385
can be as large as N: exponential
in the number of bits.

38
00:02:35,765 --> 00:02:40,314
Shor solves it with quantum interference,
as we will see in the next lesson.

39
00:02:40,694 --> 00:02:44,208
Diffusion solves it with
heat spreading over a graph.

40
00:02:44,588 --> 00:02:47,288
Both are physical ways
of detecting a period.

41
00:02:48,417 --> 00:02:52,563
This reduction matters because
RSA security depends on the

42
00:02:52,563 --> 00:02:56,423
difficulty of factoring, and
because any order-finding

43
00:02:56,423 --> 00:03:00,426
method, quantum, diffusion
or collisions, plugs into it.

44
00:03:00,806 --> 00:03:04,773
In lesson 7.4 we will use
a base with odd order.

45
00:03:05,153 --> 00:03:07,381
In lab ten you will implement it.

46
00:03:08,483 --> 00:03:11,960
Common mistakes: not computing
first the greatest common

47
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:15,064
divisor of a and N, which
may already be a factor.

48
00:03:15,444 --> 00:03:18,836
Accepting x ≡ −1, which does not factor.

49
00:03:19,216 --> 00:03:21,549
Applying the method to prime powers.

50
00:03:21,929 --> 00:03:24,841
And confusing the one-quarter
bound with the exact law.

51
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:30,207
In summary: if we know the even
order of a random number, its root

52
00:03:30,207 --> 00:03:34,012
a^(r/2) gives a factor through
the greatest common divisor.

53
00:03:34,392 --> 00:03:38,453
The Chinese remainder theorem explains
why it works most of the time.

54
00:03:38,833 --> 00:03:41,147
All the difficulty is
in finding the order.

55
00:03:42,250 --> 00:03:45,451
To practice: factor fifteen
with the order of two.

56
00:03:45,831 --> 00:03:48,928
Explain why x cannot be plus or minus one.

57
00:03:49,308 --> 00:03:52,732
Evaluate the probability for
two, three and four factors.

58
00:03:53,112 --> 00:03:56,799
And explain why direct
order search is exponential.
